  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $U(x,y,z)=(x+2){e}^{yz}$\n- $V(x,y,z)=\u27e85{e}^{-{z}^{2}},{x}^{3}y,1-{y}^{3}\u27e9$\n- $W(x,y)=\u27e8x-3{y}^{2},2-y\u27e9$",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $V(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $W(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
